<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $K$ be a $\kappa $-small simplicial set, and let $\overline{p}: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calC $ be a colimit diagram. Suppose that, for each vertex $x$ of $K$, the object $\overline{p}(x) \in \calC $ is $\kappa $-compact. We wish to show that $C= \overline{p}(\infty ) \in \calC $ is $\kappa $-compact, where $\infty $ denotes the cone point of $K^{\triangleright }$. Let $p = \overline{p}|K$, and consider the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC _{p/} \leftarrow \calC _{ \overline{p}/ } \rightarrow \calC _{ C/}.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Both are trivial fibrations (the first because $\overline{p}$ is a colimit diagram, and the second because the inclusion $\{  \infty \}  \subseteq K^{\triangleright }$ is right anodyne). Corollary <a href="/tag/3E7999" data-tag="3E7999">5.3.4.14</a> asserts that the left fibration $\calC _{p/} \rightarrow \calC $ is $\kappa $-compact. It follows that the equivalent left fibration $\calC _{C/}$ is $\kappa $-compact, so that $C$ is a $\kappa $-compact object of $\calC $ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>